uvc gadget: drop the vendor scatter-gather request path

Goke's f_uvc carries a private UVC_SG_REQ mode that replaces the mainline
request ring with a *single* in-flight usb_request (UVC_NUM_REQUESTS 1)
whose scatterlist is sized to hold an entire video frame:

	num_sgs = (video->imagesize / (req_size - 2)) + 1;

uvc_video_encode_isoc() then fills every sg entry of that one request and
hands the whole chain to the UDC in one go.

dwc3 turns each mapped sg entry into a TRB chained to the next, so the
chain describes one isochronous service interval. On a full-speed link an
isoc interval carries exactly one 1023-byte packet, so only the first sg
entry ever reaches the wire; the rest of the frame is dropped. Because the
encoder had already consumed the buffer into the sg list, the buffer is
marked done and the FID toggles, so the host sees a *complete* frame that
happens to be 1021 bytes long (1023 minus the 2-byte UVC payload header).

The visible symptom is that every frame larger than one packet arrives
truncated. H.264 decoders report a stream of

	error while decoding MB x y, bytestream -5

and roughly half the frames fail to decode, while frames that happen to
fit in a single packet come through intact.

Fall back to the mainline path: 32 plain one-packet requests kept in
flight by uvcg_video_pump(). Measured on gk7205v200 at full speed, this
takes a 1280x720 H.264 stream from ~50% corrupt frames to 120/120 frames
delivered with zero decoder errors and no gaps in the buffer sequence.

The SG code is left in place behind the now-undefined macro rather than
deleted, so it stays available for anyone who wants to revisit it on a
high-speed link where an interval can hold mult*maxpacket bytes.

diff --git a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc.h b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc.h
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc.h
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc.h
@@ -17,7 +17,12 @@
 #include <linux/types.h>
 #include <linux/usb/ch9.h>
 
-#define UVC_SG_REQ
+/* UVC_SG_REQ: the vendor scatter-gather path uses a single in-flight request
+ * carrying a whole frame as a chained TRB list. On this full-speed link the
+ * host only ever receives the first 1023-byte packet of each frame, so every
+ * frame larger than one packet arrives truncated (H.264 "bytestream -5").
+ * Fall back to the mainline path: 32 plain one-packet requests. */
+#undef UVC_SG_REQ
 #define UVC_EVENT_FIRST			(V4L2_EVENT_PRIVATE_START + 0)
 #define UVC_EVENT_CONNECT		(V4L2_EVENT_PRIVATE_START + 0)
 #define UVC_EVENT_DISCONNECT		(V4L2_EVENT_PRIVATE_START + 1)
